\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
The RSSM fidelity gap under trained conditioning is environment-dependent (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}): Walker Walk develops a $1.63\times$ reward prediction gap (MAE $0.053 \to 0.087$ at $\dt{=}2$) while observation reconstruction improves ($1.35\times$ vs.\ baseline $2.04\times$), but Cheetah Run shows \emph{no} reward gap ($1.00\times$, MAE ${\approx}0.141$ at both scales) despite exhibiting CIVO at comparable or higher severity ($8.27\times$ NVE). This dissociation indicates that reward fidelity degradation is one environment-dependent transmission pathway---operative in Walker---but not a necessary condition; in Cheetah, the trained $\dt$-embedding induces value overestimation through an alternative pathway, possibly via dynamics-model prior drift, without measurable single-step reward bias. The mechanism is analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced rather than policy-class-induced. Gradient magnitude $\neq$ causal importance: the reward head (38--49\% of $\nabla_\theta e_{\dt}$) fails when blocked alone; the observation decoder (16--22\%) carries the critical signal; full-pathway SGS (0/3) confirms forward-pass conditioning as the irreducible vulnerability. CIVO is upstream of estimation error---dual critics converge (\S\ref{sec:arch_results})---so standard corrections are inapplicable; MPC re-planning prevents accumulation (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}). On Walker, time-series analysis across 12 dense checkpoints resolves a three-stage causal chain ($n{=}2$; Appendix~\ref{sec:timeseries_fidelity}): reward fidelity degradation (SS ratio $>1.1\times$ at 20K) precedes value overestimation onset (${\sim}$30K) by ${\sim}$10K steps ($r{=}0.81$, $p{=}0.002$), which in turn precedes actor entropy explosion ${\sim}35$K steps later ($+2.1$ nats/10K in collapsed configs vs.\ $+0.07$--$0.22$ in healthy baselines; Appendix~\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}). However, entropy is a lagging indicator only: at matched time points during the CIVO onset window, collapsed and healthy runs are indistinguishable ($\Delta = 0.03$ nats at 55K; Appendix~\ref{sec:entropy_not_diagnostic}). This fidelity $\to$ value $\to$ (late) entropy chain confirms a critic-side collapse mechanism on Walker; whether Cheetah follows the same temporal ordering through its alternative (non-fidelity) pathway remains open.

\paragraph{Causal role of embedding training.}
The three-way ablation (trained/frozen/removed $\dt$-embedding; Table~\ref{tab:causal_ablation}) disambiguates two hypotheses: CIVO could arise from the architectural presence of a $\dt$-conditioning pathway, or specifically from learned $\dt$-specific representations shaped by gradient flow. Since frozen embeddings participate in the forward pass identically to trained ones yet do not trigger CIVO (0/13 frozen or removed controls vs.\ 6/7 trained, $p < 0.001$; NVE ${\leq}\,0.53\times$ in all four cross-environment frozen seeds), the vulnerability lies in what the embedding \emph{learns}---near-antipodal representations (Figure~\ref{fig:cosine_similarity}; temporal dynamics in \S\ref{sec:cosine_temporal}) that create exploitable structure through environment-dependent pathways (reward fidelity degradation in Walker, alternative transmission in Cheetah; Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity})---rather than in conditioning architecture per se. For world-model RL systems using DreamerV3's RSSM, this suggests that auxiliary conditioning signals whose gradient flow touches the dynamics model require fidelity guarantees matching the model's prediction accuracy; without such guarantees, learned conditioning creates exploitable structure that imagination-based critics cannot self-correct.

\paragraph{Intervention resistance.}
Seven targeted interventions across four levels fail to reliably eliminate CIVO (Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}): gradient stopping shifts co-adaptation to unblocked pathways---obs-decoder-only SGS on Cheetah \emph{amplifies} NVE from $8.3\times$ to $21.9$--$30.2\times$ (compensatory amplification; blocking one pathway intensifies co-adaptation through the remainder). Output capping (RAVE) is defeated by its own EMA anchor tracking biased values. Representation noise (TCN) exhibits an impossible-dose dilemma: the effective dose ($\sigma{=}1.0$ on Cheetah) suppresses CIVO entirely (NVE${\leq}0.3\times$) but erodes policy to $15\%$ of baseline through cumulative actor gradient interference, while lower doses ($\sigma{=}0.2$) succeed on only 2/4 Walker seeds and 0/3 cross-environment. The pattern indicates pathway redundancy---CIVO exploits whichever gradient channel remains open, and partial interventions can worsen severity---consistent with it being a systemic property of imagination-based training with temporal conditioning rather than a defect in any single component.

\paragraph{Practitioner guidelines.}
Monitor via NVE diagnostic ($\geq 1.5\times$, AUROC${=}0.990$, F1${=}0.941$ at $n{=}56$ across two environments; Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}). Freezing the $\dt$-embedding eliminates CIVO (0/13 frozen or removed controls; cross-environment verification: 4/4 frozen seeds across Walker $+$ Cheetah, all NVE $< 0.53\times$); untreated, extreme CIVO ($70.6\times$) causes irrecoverable damage (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}).

\paragraph{Training horizon.}
Our primary analysis uses 105K steps, sufficient to observe CIVO onset (45K--65K) and classify trajectories. Extended training (${\sim}$302K steps, one seed) shows complete self-recovery after approximately 170K additional steps post-onset---at substantial computational cost that the frozen $\dt$-embedding diagnostic eliminates entirely (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_training}).

