\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline reaches 915.7 at 105K; fixed $\dt{=}2$ (006) peaks at 485.3 before collapsing to 132.9 ($14\%$ of baseline); fixed $\dt{=}4$ (007) never exceeds 176.9 ($19\%$); the learned gate (005) collapses to 98.8 ($11\%$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves. The baseline (black) reaches 915.7; all variable-rate configurations degrade.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Precedes Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the strongest predictor. The \emph{primary} comparison---008d (trained, subsample ON) vs.\ 008f (no embedding, subsample ON)---differs only in embedding training, yielding $\nve = 4.12\times$ (eval 82.8) vs.\ $\nve \leq 1.69\times$ (eval 139.5--468.8$^f$). \emph{Secondary} comparisons against 008a/008c (unexposed embedding) co-vary subsample status and horizon alongside the embedding factor. The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold (35K+ window) correctly classifies 53 of 56 experimental configurations across two environments ($n{=}56$; AUROC${=}0.990$, F1${=}0.941$; Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}).
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{NVE trajectories (mean $\pm$ seed range). Trained $\dt$-embedding (red, Walker Walk, $n{=}3$ seeds plotted; seeds~4 and~5 data in Table~\ref{tab:complete_results}) exceeds the $1.5\times$ threshold (dashed) in all seeds; no-embedding control (blue) remains near $1.0\times$. Three trajectory types emerge: early collapse (seed~0), late-onset collapse (seed~3), and self-correction (seed~2). Dotted line marks the 35K diagnostic window onset.}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

The trained $\dt$-embedding precedes CIVO in all five Walker Walk seeds (peak NVE $1.53$--$4.72\times$), universally degrading performance to below 30\% of baseline (IQM $148.8$, $n{=}5$; \citealt{agarwal2021precipice}). Including replications and Cheetah Run (\S\ref{sec:f6}), CIVO onset occurs in 6 of 7 tested multi-joint locomotion runs (86\%, two environments). Sustained collapse occurs in 4/7 runs; two runs self-correct and one shows onset without sustained collapse (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

\paragraph{Mechanism evidence.}
On Walker Walk, the RSSM reward head develops a $1.63\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning (reward MAE $0.053 \to 0.087$; Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), while observation reconstruction improves (gap $1.35\times$ vs.\ baseline $2.04\times$). However, Cheetah Run exhibits no reward gap ($1.00\times$) despite comparable CIVO severity ($8.27\times$ NVE), indicating that reward fidelity degradation is one transmission pathway but not a necessary condition. Direct value calibration confirms the critic internalizes this bias: the CIVO model's mean $V(s)/\text{MC-return}$ ratio reaches $7.80\times$ (median $3.40\times$; 100\% of sampled states overestimated) vs.\ median $1.05\times$ for the nearly-calibrated control (52.5\% overestimated). Causal ordering further supports a value-channel pathology: evaluation return collapses ${\sim}35$K steps before actor entropy rises ($+2.1$ nats/10K in collapsed configurations vs.\ $+0.07$--$0.22$ in healthy baselines; Appendix~\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}).

\paragraph{Temporal ordering ($n{=}2$).}
Time-series analysis across 12 dense checkpoints (10K--65K steps) provides evidence that reward fidelity degradation precedes value overestimation onset: the single-step fidelity ratio ($\text{MAE}_{\dt{=}2}/\text{MAE}_{\dt{=}1}$) crosses $1.1\times$ at 20K steps, while the value-to-eval ratio begins rising at ${\sim}$30K (reaching $1.59\times$ at 40K)---a ${\sim}$10K onset-to-onset lag ($r{=}0.81$, $p{=}0.002$; Figure~\ref{fig:temporal_ordering}). A second seed (exp\_066b) confirms the pattern: fidelity gap onset at ${\sim}$15K precedes VE onset at ${\sim}$24K (temporal lead ${\approx}$9K steps, consistent with seed~0's ${\approx}$10K), though seed~1's peak VE ($0.72\times$) remained below the $1.5\times$ threshold within 60K steps, suggesting seed-dependent progression rates. The gap arises from differential improvement: $\dt{=}1$ MAE decreases continuously ($0.093 \to 0.047$) while $\dt{=}2$ MAE plateaus (${\sim}0.08$; Appendix~\ref{sec:timeseries_fidelity}).

%% ============================================================
%% F3--F5: Summary (details in Appendix B)
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

Trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly sufficient in tested configurations} for CIVO. Neither factor alone triggers the CIVO feedback loop: trained embedding without multi-rate exposure (008a, Walker 3 seeds, peak NVE $0.83$--$0.93\times$; Cheetah 2 seeds, NVE $0.45$--$0.58\times$) and multi-rate exposure without embedding (008f, 4 seeds, peak NVE $\leq 1.69\times$$^f$) all remain below $\tau{=}2.0\times$ across both environments, confirming that untrained or absent $\dt$-embedding blocks the CIVO feedback loop. Performance varies by environment and horizon (Walker $82$--$93\%$ baseline at $H{=}8$; Cheetah $63$--$82\%$ (mean $73\%$) at $H{=}15$), consistent with longer imagination horizons compounding small untrained-embedding errors; the mechanism-level conclusion (NVE $\ll \tau$, no CIVO) holds uniformly. Freezing the embedding (\texttt{requires\_grad=False}) eliminates CIVO: across 6 Walker frozen seeds (NVE $0.19$--$0.53\times$ in 4/6, transient spikes $1.71$--$1.88\times$ in 2/6; Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb}) and 4 Cheetah frozen seeds (all NVE $< 1.35\times$), no frozen run exhibits sustained CIVO. A controlled $2{\times}2$ frozen verification (Walker $+$ Cheetah, 2 seeds each; exp\_084--087, all NVE $< 0.53\times$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}) confirms that gradient training---not embedding presence---is necessary. Across all runs, trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO in 6/7 multi-joint locomotion seeds versus 0/13 frozen or removed controls (Fisher exact $p < 0.001$). Partial gradient blocking (exp\_044) does not prevent onset (peak NVE $4.45\times$) but permits delayed self-correction (final eval 538, 59\% baseline; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}), confirming that \emph{any} gradient pathway updating $e_{\dt}$ suffices to induce CIVO. Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$; Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}).

\paragraph{ManiSkill manipulation tasks.}
None of three ManiSkill environments exhibit $\dt$-embedding-dependent CIVO. All value-ratio spikes ($4$--$11\times$) are environment-specific: frozen controls show comparable or higher peaks (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}), and all self-correct. CIVO is specific to multi-joint locomotion in DreamerV3; the frozen-embedding diagnostic correctly identifies these transients as non-CIVO.

%% ============================================================
%% F6--F7: Interventions
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F6--F7: Interventions}
\label{sec:f6}

\paragraph{F6: $\dt{=}1$ imagination eliminates CIVO.}
Exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding. Across four unique seeds (s0, s1, s2, s5), none exhibits CIVO onset (peak NVE ${\leq}0.8\times$, well below the $1.5\times$ threshold). Three of four seeds achieve $64$--$94\%$ of baseline (eval $597$--$877$; mean $775.6$); seed~0 exhibits CIVO-unrelated learning stagnation (eval $148$, $16\%$ baseline; Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}). This confirms that multi-step imagination---not the trained embedding alone---is necessary for CIVO induction.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}
\caption{Contrasting dynamics. \textbf{Left:} 008d (CIVO), value explosion with entropy decrease. \textbf{Right:} 008e\_v3 s1 ($\dt{=}1$ imagination), no CIVO onset (NVE ${\leq}\,0.6\times$, eval $94\%$ baseline).}
\label{fig:multiple_pathways}
\end{figure}
\paragraph{F7: Full-pathway gradient stopping shows mixed results.}
Full-SGS (exp\_018) detaches all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$. Seed~1 shows partial mitigation (eval 448.3, NVE $1.66\times$); seed~2 collapses (0/2 elimination; Appendix~\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}). Cross-environment validation on Cheetah (010b, NVE $8.27$--$108.52\times$) confirms CIVO generalization; full-SGS yields 0/3 elimination. Removing the embedding eliminates CIVO on Cheetah (024b, NVE $< 0.2\times$).

\paragraph{Targeted obs-decoder-only SGS confirms pathway redundancy.}
Obs-decoder-only SGS (exp\_025/026) achieves 1/6 CIVO prevention (1/4 Walker, 0/2 Cheetah; Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}), not meaningfully different from full-pathway SGS (0/3). CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels: blocking any single pathway shifts co-adaptation to the remaining ones (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

