%% ============================================================
%% Forward-pass interventions
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}
\label{sec:forward_results}

We test two forward-pass alternatives (Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}). \textbf{RAVE} (value-level capping) fails: the EMA anchor tracks biased values during CIVO onset. \textbf{TCN} (representation-level noise) suppresses CIVO in 2 of 4 Walker seeds at $\sigma{=}0.2$ (NVE $< 1.0\times$, eval 45--91\%), but remaining seeds show runaway escalation, suggesting TCN gates rather than attenuates CIVO. Cross-environment transfer fails: Cheetah effective dose ($\sigma{=}1.0$) causes policy erosion to 15\% baseline; PickCube 0/1 (Appendix~\ref{sec:tcn_cross_env}). TCN is a \emph{diagnostic probe}, not a deployable mitigation.

%% ============================================================
%% Architecture-Level Ablation
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Architecture-Level Ablation}
\label{sec:arch_results}

\paragraph{Critic convergence in pure imagination.}
Across all dual-critic configurations ($n{=}7$), critics trained on shared RSSM features converge ($\rho > 0.999$), with pessimism at 3--4\% of the independent-critic bound (\S\ref{sec:dual_critic_method}). Pessimistic estimation is thus ineffective; the min operation in wide dual-critic may provide regularization through mechanisms that remain to be characterized (Appendix~\ref{sec:dual_critic_appendix}).

\paragraph{$2{\times}2$ factorial: recovery vs.\ prevention.}
Min drives recovery: narrow dual-critic (${\sim}1.2$M) recovers fully (${\sim}16$--$19\times$ recovery ratio) despite $2\times$ fewer parameters. Prevention requires min $+$ capacity: wide dual-critic maintains $\nve \leq 1.4\times$ (3/3 prevention, 1/3 non-CIVO collapse). Cross-environment transfer is limited (Appendix~\ref{sec:dual_critic_appendix}).

%% ============================================================
%% Online CIVO Detection
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Online CIVO Detection}
\label{sec:detector}

We derive a lightweight online detector monitoring the value-to-evaluation ratio for superlinear growth (Algorithm~\ref{alg:civo_detector}). Applied to 14 runs across three environments, it achieves TPR${=}5/5$ (95\% Clopper-Pearson CI $[47.8\%, 100\%]$), FPR${=}2/9$ ($22\%$), with predictive alerts in 2/5 cases (Appendix~\ref{sec:detector_appendix}). The detector requires larger-sample validation before deployment as a practical diagnostic tool.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Online CIVO Detector. Monitors the value-to-evaluation ratio $r_t = \bar{V}_t / \bar{E}_t$ (not NVE) for superlinear growth indicative of CIVO onset.}
\label{alg:civo_detector}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Stream of $(\bar{V}_t, \bar{E}_t)$ at each checkpoint $t$
\STATE $\tau_\text{mag} \leftarrow 0.49$, \quad $\tau_\text{vel} \leftarrow 0.1$, \quad $\varepsilon \leftarrow 10^{-6}$
\STATE Initialize $r_\text{prev} \leftarrow 0$, $\Delta r_\text{prev} \leftarrow 0$, $\mathcal{H} \leftarrow []$
\FOR{each checkpoint $t$}
  \STATE $r_t \leftarrow \bar{V}_t \,/\, \max(\bar{E}_t,\, \varepsilon)$
  \STATE $\Delta r_t \leftarrow r_t - r_\text{prev}$
  \STATE $c_\text{mag} \leftarrow (r_t \geq \tau_\text{mag})$
  \STATE $c_\text{vel} \leftarrow (\Delta r_t > \tau_\text{vel})$
  \STATE $c_\text{acc} \leftarrow (\Delta r_\text{prev} > \tau_\text{vel}) \wedge (\Delta r_t > \Delta r_\text{prev})$
  \STATE $c_\text{dec} \leftarrow \bigl|\{i : \mathcal{H}[i] < \mathcal{H}[i{-}1],\; i \in \text{last 3}\}\bigr| \geq 2$
  \IF{$c_\text{mag} \wedge c_\text{vel} \wedge c_\text{acc} \wedge c_\text{dec}$}
    \STATE \textbf{Alert:} CIVO onset at step $t$
  \ENDIF
  \STATE $r_\text{prev} \leftarrow r_t$, \quad $\Delta r_\text{prev} \leftarrow \Delta r_t$
  \STATE Append $\bar{E}_t$ to $\mathcal{H}$
\ENDFOR
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

